@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# Embedded-host integration patch
+
+Based on upstream pi-chrome 0.15.51 at commit
+4811ee616d2bbd5ead669a7e2f33a0bb14bd43ef. This patch is a maintained integration,
+not a claim that these changes were accepted upstream. Upstream MIT license
+continues to apply. Native tools, schemas, observations, background-target
+selection and tab ownership remain the upstream implementation.
+
+```ts
+import { createChromeExtension, probeChromeConnection } from "./index.ts";
+export default createChromeExtension({
+  embedded: true,
+  companionPath: "/stable/machine-local/Pi Chrome Connector",
+});
+```
+
+`embedded: true` registers the native tools for every Pi session, including
+catalog-only loads. It uses installation as authority, without `/chrome
+authorize` prompts. The standalone default retains upstream authorization and
+background-mode behavior. User-facing host enable/disable stays with the native
+package/resource lifecycle; it must fence new sessions and dispose old sessions
+through Pi's lifecycle rather than attempting to fence arbitrary full-trust code.
+
+No bridge starts on embedded factory construction or `session_start`. Explicit
+first tool use or connection inspection starts it. A process-shared transport
+has per-session leases and no shared authorization. Session shutdown cancels its
+requests, attempts bounded cleanup of only its tracked creations/adoptions, and
+releases its transport lease; another embedded session keeps its own tools and
+connection. Reload preserves the session's browser targets. Existing user tabs
+are ungrouped when appropriate, never closed by cleanup; tests retain upstream
+ownership behavior.
+
+`probeChromeConnection({host?, port?, timeoutMs?})` is an explicit connection
+check which dispatches only `tab.version`. It never reads page content, creates a
+tab, groups a tab, navigates or runs JavaScript in a page. Factory/catalog loading
+must not call it. The upstream `/chrome doctor` command intentionally runs page
+probes; do not use doctor as automatic readiness inspection.
+
+## Companion distribution
+
+Ship the matching browser-extension folder at a stable path outside the versioned
+application bundle, retaining license/provenance. The person loads this folder
+once in their Chrome profile with Chrome's normal extension UI. Browser extension
+permission setup is not a model tool. Future updates must copy the matching
+companion to that same stable path and provide a user-accessible Reload/Check
+connection action. Never silently edit Chrome's profile or install an unrelated
+browser extension.
+
+Protocol 2 uses `/next-v2`, `/command-v2` and `/command-state-v2`. Poll requests
+carry the exact companion package version and protocol version; the broker
+rejects mismatches before dequeuing. A versioned endpoint makes old standalone
+brokers fail without executing requests whose extra fields they might ignore.
+Do not run the old companion against this bridge or claim it is ready. Old
+brokers occupying the port must be updated/stopped before this integration works.
+The companion no longer reloads itself after the host has already dequeued a
+command. Matching metadata from `tab.version` is required for readiness.
+
+## Cancellation and uncertainty
+
+Queued requests are withdrawn on cancellation. Forwarded HTTP request disconnects
+cancel the owner's queue entry. A dispatched request loses its command lease on
+Stop, timeout or owner loss; the worker checks the lease before dispatch and every
+200ms while working, stops further Chrome calls, and detaches only targets touched
+by that command. The worker does not begin another command until its previous
+JavaScript dispatcher has drained. User cancellation of the debugger withdraws the
+current command too.
+
+Already-submitted browser effects cannot be undone by AbortSignal. Results say
+that the outcome may be unknown and require inspection before deciding to repeat
+an action. Broker owner promotion never repeats the failed request. CDP recovery
+retries only a precise pre-dispatch `Debugger is not attached` failure; ambiguous
+detach/closed-target failures never replay input or evaluation.
+
+There is no continuous desktop capture. Page-load feedback instrumentation runs
+only for the active command's resolved tab, not on every navigation in the user's
+profile. Native screenshot output and model feedback behavior remain unchanged.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` runs the existing upstream suites plus embedded-host acceptance. The
+additional tests use the real maintained factory/broker, loopback HTTP with a fake
+companion, and the real worker functions with mocked Chrome APIs. They exercise
+catalog-before-two-sessions, lazy/ref-counted startup, owner shutdown, protocol
+mismatch, forwarded and delivered cancellation, unknown effects without replay,
+worker drain, touched-target cleanup, setup probes, startup/Stop races and idle
+navigation. No real Chrome profile or user tab is modified by this lane.
+
+These tests establish integration behavior, not packaged Chrome readiness. The
+host release must still verify stable companion loading, real user-tab preservation,
+two concurrent Chats, live model feedback and cancellation through its signed app.
+
+Embedded hosts provide `getCompanionToken`, a lazy reader for a 32-byte hex credential
+held in host-owned state. Catalog creation never reads or creates credentials or opens
+ports. The materialized companion's `host-config.json` is `{version:1, mode:"embedded",
+token:...}`; ordinary upstream companions retain `{version:1, mode:"standalone"}`.
+All embedded companion endpoints authenticate `x-pi-chrome-companion`; embedded
+HTTP command ingress is disabled even with that credential. Tools send in process.
+An occupied port fails without forwarding. Commands, lease checks and results carry
+a bridge epoch; automation ownership also includes that epoch, so a fresh host cannot
+inherit prior-launch targets. Neither credentials nor epochs are authorization prompts.
+Credentials must stay outside project folders and model/renderer context.
+
+In embedded mode `chrome_screenshot` returns native image blocks and capture
+provenance. It does not write a project screenshot file unless the caller supplies
+an output path. Full-page captures return at most 16 tiles and 16 MiB of decoded
+image data; explicit exports retain image results. Native Pi session persistence
+still records the model's tool-result images according to its normal session policy.
+
+
+## Published companion bootstrap
+
+An optional `getConnection` host callback replaces the file token with a host-owned
+`{connectionId, leaseToken, extensionOrigin, bridge:{major,minor,capabilities}}`.
+The exact native extension origin ends in `/`; HTTP compares its normalized origin.
+`onConnectionRevoked` fences every queue/result and drops the physical transport.
+`reportConnectionObservation` reports authenticated compatible polling only.
+`beginWork(connectionId)` holds the host's affected-work fence until `agent_end`
+or session disposal, including pauses between tool calls. The host must refuse
+profile changes while that work is active. These callbacks are optional; ordinary
+standalone Pi and manually prepared protocol2 companions retain their behavior.
+
+`startChromeConnection(options)` retains a transport for an explicitly selected
+connection even when no Pi session is using it; close releases only that owner.
+Factories/catalog reads remain cold. `probeChromeConnection(options)` sends only
+`tab.version`. Revocation never replays a command or silently transfers a turn.
+Protocol3 requires the exact selected origin, token and connection ID, then checks
+protocol/capabilities before dequeue. Companion version may update independently
+from the host package. The app distributes its static Store bootstrap separately;
+no per-user token is placed in that archive. Native messaging authenticates the
+extension ID, not the profile: selection and proof belong to the host bootstrap.
